    <!-- ============ FIRST-RUN WELCOME ============
         Shown once, before the app proper. Screen one is the only place ALIVE
         lets itself poetry; screens two and three are mechanics and switches,
         kept plain - an onboarding poem that leaves the rules unstated is just
         a nicer-looking version of the confusion it was meant to fix. -->
    <string name="welcome_lead">You keep starting over. That is not weakness — it is the only way out.</string>
    <string name="welcome_body">Relapse does not announce itself. One clip, one more round — and the night is gone, again. Deciding harder has never held. So ALIVE stops asking you to decide: it closes the door you already chose to close, and hands the time back only for something real.</string>
    <string name="welcome_note">A door, not a scoreboard.</string>
    <string name="welcome_loop_heading">How it works</string>
    <string name="welcome_loop_1">Every day starts with 120 minutes of entertainment.</string>
    <string name="welcome_loop_2">When the minutes run out, your entertainment apps close. Chat and work stay open.</string>
    <string name="welcome_loop_3">One offline quest buys the day back — its minutes go to the Time Bank, and up to 20 can return to today.</string>
    <string name="welcome_loop_4">The day a quest is finished is a kept day. Kept days add up.</string>
    <string name="welcome_loop_note">The accessibility service can be switched off. ALIVE will say so — and the day is marked unguarded.</string>
    <string name="welcome_permissions_heading">Two switches before anything works</string>
    <string name="welcome_usage_label">Usage access</string>
    <string name="welcome_accessibility_label">Accessibility</string>
    <string name="welcome_usage_why">Usage access is how ALIVE counts minutes in entertainment apps — totals, never content.</string>
    <string name="welcome_next">Next</string>
    <string name="welcome_start">Start</string>
    <string name="welcome_skip">Skip for now</string>

